# Tests

The test layers are in `docs/design.ja.md` §10. This rule is how a test is written.

## Framework

- xUnit v3 with its built-in `Assert`. No assertion library.
- Tests run on Microsoft.Testing.Platform (`global.json` `test.runner`). The .NET 10 SDK refuses VSTest for these projects, so there is no `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` or `xunit.runner.visualstudio`. Run one project with `dotnet test --project <path>`, and some of its tests with `dotnet test --project <path> -- --filter-method <pattern>`. The test executable takes xUnit's own options (`-method`); given `--filter-method`, it prints `error: unknown option`, runs no test, and exits 3, which a loop that looks only for failed tests counts as a pass. Read the exit code and the count of tests run.
- A test that reads a file of the repository finds the repository by walking up from `AppContext.BaseDirectory` to `HyperPlot.slnx`, not from `[CallerFilePath]`: the CI build (`ContinuousIntegrationBuild`) maps source paths to `/_/`, and PR #47's shader hash test failed on both runners while it passed here.
- Before calling a failing test a defect, look for what a mutation run leaves (`git status`, `*.orig` beside the source) and build again. A test that fails on a build that still has a mutant in it points to a defect that does not exist.
- A test checked by a mutant reads the mutant's build first: a mutant that does not compile leaves the last binary, and its tests pass. Under warnings as errors, a mutant the compiler warns about does not compile: `if (false)` is unreachable code (CS0162). Write the mutant as a condition the compiler cannot fold (`if (_disposed && ...)`). PR #288's first mutant was `if (false)`, its build output was thrown away, and the mutant looked as if it survived. A field the mutant leaves unread does not compile either (CS0414), as in PR #320. A mutant's run is read by its exit code and the count of tests run, not by the count of failures: PR #320's mutant that freed a mipmap at once crashed the test process on the freed memory (0xC0000005), the run printed `失敗: 0` with no test passed, and it looked as if it survived.
- A test of an invariant (`InvariantViolationException`) runs in the Debug configuration only, because release builds end the process. Start it with a `RequireDebugBuild()` helper that calls `Assert.Skip` when `DEBUG` is not defined. The gate runs both configurations; an invariant test without the skip ends the Release test process at its first assertion.
- Process-wide state (a WPF `Dispatcher`, the native handle table, a window, a D3D11 device shared by a fixture) is touched only from one dedicated thread per test assembly (a fixture). xUnit v3 runs each test assembly in its own process.
- Time and frames come from a fake `TimeProvider` and the test frame scheduler (design §8.3, §10). A test never sleeps, never waits on the wall clock, and never asserts what the clock decides.
- The exception is a test of how the OS or the host itself waits and wakes (a message loop, WPF's rendering event). It is marked `[Trait("Category", "OsTiming")]`, waits in real time with bounds an order of magnitude above what was measured, and has a watchdog that wakes and fails a loop that does not finish, so that it cannot hang the run.
- Other state outside the test that the code under test reads, as it reads the clock (the keys the thread's keyboard state holds down, a setting of the process), is set by the test before each piece of work, not left as the machine has it. A WPF view reads `Keyboard.Modifiers`, which comes from the test thread's keyboard state; read with a modifier down, a drag, a wheel turn, or a hover went to another tool than the one bound without modifiers, and four input tests of `HyperPlotViewTests` failed with plain assertions (Issue #564). PR #587's `WpfThread.Run` sets Shift, Ctrl, and Alt up in that state (`SetKeyboardState`, which reaches no other thread) before each piece of work, and the four tests' messages name the modifiers read. That the failures of 2026-11-14 had this cause was not shown: a modifier set down on purpose failed the same four tests with the same messages, and 80 runs without it failed none.
- The z-order of windows is state outside the test too. WPF picks the element under the mouse with `WindowFromPoint` at the screen point (`MouseDevice.GlobalHitTest`), so a test that sends a Win32 mouse message makes its window topmost and asserts that the window at that point is its own, naming the window there when it is not. In Issue #604 the Windows runner's own terminal window lay above the test window in some runs (8 of 20), and `Disposed_UnderTheMouse_TakesTheLeave` failed each time; with the window topmost it failed in none of 20 (Fisher's exact test, p = 0.003, PR #629). A runner image that differs between runs is ruled out by the images of the failing runs, not by one pass: the failing runs had two images, and a passing run had one of them.
- A test that wakes a loop or a worker from another thread owns that thread and joins it before the test ends. A wake queued to the thread pool outlives the test and reaches the next one, which runs in another order on CI than locally. A test that waits for its own wake also counts and asserts the iterations before it, so a stray wake fails loudly instead of being measured.
- A loop checks a test's done condition only when it wakes. When another thread sets that condition, it wakes the loop after setting it; otherwise the loop stays blocked and the watchdog fails the test.
- A test that draws until work on a worker is done is bounded in time, not in frames: "draw until no frame is provisional, fail after 60 s", not "draw at most 200 frames". A PR that moves work onto a worker searches the tests for loops bounded by a count of frames and changes them. In PR #614 the workers came to read the point pages, and `IndexedPointHitTests.Show`, 200 frames with an engine that does not wait for its indexes, ended before the reads on the Windows Debug runner of CI; `58abef8` made it the file's `Settle`.
- A test that checks an answer that depends on a worker's result (a hit, a drawn layer, a count of allocations) after a fixed number of frames makes its engine wait for its indexes (`WaitsForIndexes = true`; `PlotEngine` does not wait by default) and asserts that the first frame is not provisional, or draws until no frame is provisional. On a loaded machine the shared worker pool comes late, and a pending layer is skipped: `PointHitPanelTests.SeriesBarsAndSelection_KeepAnsweringOutsideTheView` found no hit once in a whole-project run (Issue #637), and `ScriptRecordingTests.Frames_OfADeepZoom_AllocateNothing` drew a rule for the first time in a counted frame (Issue #634). Holding the one worker of a pool of 1 and letting it go at chosen points reproduced both.
- A wait in the product that repeats until other work is done (the headless render waiting for reads) has an exit when a round makes no progress, written with the wait, and its tests have a watchdog. PR #614's review found three ways in which the headless render never returned (a read asked of a disposed pool, a read left after a cache's detach, a read put back without waking its pool); the fixes added the exit and the watchdogs after.
- A tool that a script runs (`dotnet` from Python's `subprocess`, a mutant runner) is called by its full path when the SDK is not the machine's own: a child process looks the name up on its own `PATH`. After the machine's .NET 10 SDK was removed, PR #614's first batch of mutants found the removed SDK through `subprocess` (exit 0x8000811B) and did not run, though the shell's `PATH` named the per-user SDK.
- A test that reads pixels or places content by coordinates asserts the size of the render target first: a window's outer size is not its client size, and a WPF element's size is in device-independent pixels.
- A test that reads positions inside the panel asserts the panel's rectangle first, or draws a scene whose guides take no room. A guide that a layer brings with it changes the panel: in PR #201, a raster of numbers brought a color bar, the panel narrowed, and x came out half of what the test expected; the geometry tests now draw RGBA rasters, which have no color bar.
- A test of an object bound to the thread that made it (`PlotEngine`, a host's control) does not `await` a task that object returns: the continuation runs on the thread that finished the task, a worker, and the object's thread check throws there. Wait on the test's thread (`AsTask().GetAwaiter().GetResult()` in a helper), as PR #369's `SelectedRows` does.
- `TestContext.Current` is async-local and does not flow to a fixture's dedicated thread. Read `TestContext.Current.TestOutputHelper` on the test thread before handing work to that thread. To see the output of a passing test, run the test executable with `-showLiveOutput`.
- A test that needs an OS component (a font, a GPU feature level) skips, with its name, when the machine lacks it, and calls that check for each one it assumes, so that it does not fail on Server Core or a runner without the component. A test that can skip on the machine (a missing component, an OS notice that does not come) is shown to have run on CI by the counts the run's log gives for its project (`skipped: 0` where no other test of the project skips), read for each configuration, not by how long the run took: a skipped test passes CI and guards nothing. PR #588's new WPF test skips when no loss of the front buffer comes within 10 s (2 runs of 20 here); its first comment inferred that it had run from the project's 6.1 s and 3.7 s, the review asked for the evidence, and the logs read `total: 54`, `failed: 0`, `skipped: 0` in Release and in Debug.
- Tests that need a GPU use WARP and run on Windows only; they are marked so the other layers run on every OS. Tests of `HyperPlot.Wpf` run on Windows only.
- A Core type that the tests on Linux make calls no Windows API and no native library in its constructor or field initializers; such a call goes where the code that needs it runs. The local gate runs on Windows only, so such a call first fails in CI, in every test that makes the type.
- Code whose shape depends on a size (a buffer grown for the largest so far, a texture made for the viewport, a pyramid with one level per factor) is tested at the small end too, and with the small case first: an empty column, one point, one chunk, a viewport of one pixel.
- WARP golden images and draw IR (`RenderList`) snapshots render with the bundled fonts only (design §10).
- A WARP golden image is compared with `GoldenImage` of `HyperPlot.Testing` (design §10.1); its baseline is `Golden/<name>.png` under the test project, in git. A baseline is written only by a run with `HYPERPLOT_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1`, and a PR that changes one says why in its body; the reviewer looks at the image in the diff. A test never writes a missing baseline by itself, and a tolerance names the measured difference it covers.
- A CPU reference writes a pixel's four bytes in four statements, not one tuple assignment: in the Release build of the D3D11 tests the tuple form left R at 0 in every pixel (Issue #117). Which tests it breaks depends on the order of the run: `LineTests.DeviceLost_NextFrame_DrawsTheSamePixels` failed alone on main and passed in the whole project, and failed in the whole project once PR #439 added tests (Issues #287 and #99).
- A golden scene drawn with the fake text backend shows each character as a hatched box, which no one can read. A PR that adds or rewrites a scene with text draws it once with DirectWrite and looks at which label stands under which mark. Scene 13 drew a band axis's labels a category to the left of their bars from row G7b on; it was found only when PR #189 drew scene 16 with DirectWrite (Issue #190).
- Before a failure is taken for one that comes now and then, check whether it repeats and after which change it began. A failure in every local gate is a defect to bisect, not noise to rerun.
- A test that allocates or maps tens of megabytes (a large column, a deep pyramid) runs in a collection apart from the others (`DisableParallelization`), and a large input it needs is built in pieces or generated into a mapped file rather than made as one array. Next to a Zero-Alloc test, its garbage makes that test count kilobytes with no collection.
- The warm-up of a Zero-Alloc test over a cache that overwrites its oldest entry runs the measured loops as they are measured. Which entry holds which key depends on the order of the runs before, and an entry grows its arrays the first time it holds a larger need. One run that allocates nothing does not end the warm-up: a later run can still allocate once (a member plot's first ticks at a view). Print each run's bytes for a dozen runs first, warm for as many runs as that shows, and count two. PR #379's linked wheel allocated 0 B in its second run and 4,944 B in its third, then nothing.
- A Zero-Alloc test whose iteration takes an index from the measuring helper uses the index range the helper documents (its unmeasured window may hand negative indices), continues the warm-up's run of indices, and counts the work the iterations claim to do (frames presented, buckets written).
- A Zero-Alloc test of a plan draws, besides the same spec again, specs a host would build anew each frame: new nodes of the same looks, and other domains in turn. PR #173's test drew the same spec only; a harness that built its spec each frame found that a split layer made its parts' nodes again, about 350 bytes a series a frame. PR #247's facet test made its second spec with `with`, so the facet's node stayed the same; the measurement's harness built each spec anew and found the arrangement made again for a new node of the same values, 272 bytes a frame.
- A Zero-Alloc test's own helpers allocate nothing in the measured window: a handler that keeps the notices it gets in a list reserves the list's room first. PR #275's engine helper kept each `ViewChanged` in a `List`, and its doubling showed as 34 KB a round, which was nearly taken for the engine's.
- A test of an animation includes input that comes at each frame, as a wheel turned on: the event and the frame at the same time. A test that zooms once and draws frames after misses an animation that starts again at each event. PR #275's animation restarted from the last frame's value at each turn, so the view did not move while the wheel turned; the tests passed, and the 1e9-point measurement found it.
- A path that returns before recording (a target too small, nothing to draw) is tested on a plan that drew before, and checks that the reused output is empty. A new plan starts empty and hides output left from the frame before: in PR #259, a figure too small for its cells returned early and the renderer drew the previous figure's cells and title, while the test used a new plan.
- A test that a part is skipped (a hidden series, a facet out of view) checks something the skipped path would do besides its pixels, such as a function that must not be called: a skipped part often draws no pixel either way.
- A test of what asks for a frame changes one kind of state after another (a data append, then a style change, then a zoom), not each alone on a fresh plot.
- A clue added to a test that fails now and then lives in the test, not in shipped code, and is checked once with a failure caused on purpose. Shipped code gets at most an internal read of state the test cannot reach (marked "Tests only"); the clue's logic, its waits, and its message stay in the test. PR #303 added three such reads (`D3D11SharedSurface.Describe`, `HyperPlotView.SharedSurface`, `FrontBufferAvailable`) and kept the rest in `AssertShown`.
- An assertion's failure message names the expectation that failed, in the words of the test's Expected: `Assert.False(atlas.TryFind(...), "the first glyph of the first shelf was not taken out")`. PR #649 checked `Assert.True(atlas.TryFind(...) is false, ...)` with "the first glyph of the first shelf is still held", a state, and the review read it as the opposite of the condition.
- A clue for a result that a branch decides says each of the branch's conditions, not only the branch taken. PR #303's first clue said that the view had left the shared textures; which of its three conditions (the backend's adapter, Direct3D 9Ex's device, the front buffer) was false took a second push.
- A test that checks that something waits for WPF's rendering tick, right after it pumps the dispatcher, accepts that the tick drew the frame in the pump: "waits or drew", counted by frames drawn. PR #306 fixed three such checks that raced WPF's own tick.
- A counter a test reads counts the event after it happened: PR #306 counted a frame before drawing it, so a frame a device loss stopped counted as drawn.
- Every run of `./build.ps1` writes its output to a file in the scratchpad, so that a failure seen once can be named.

## Structure

- `tests/<Project>.Tests` mirrors `src/<Project>`. `Foo.cs` → `FooTests.cs`, in the same folder.
- Method names: `{Target}_{Scenario}_{Expected}` in PascalCase, English.
- A test that tells two cases apart (a shared and a free domain, a run and the whole column) uses data on which they differ, and its expected values are computed from that data, not written in the form the code computes. PR #247's regions all had the same times, so a free x and a shared x learned the same domain, and a helper that added the code's widening to both sides hid it; a mutant that learned every panel's x from all panels survived until the regions' x were moved apart.
- Each test owns its state. Tests run in parallel; a test that cannot (a real window, a fixture's thread, a large mapped file) says why in its collection definition.

## Documentation

Every `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` has XML docs in this shape, in English:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// One line: the behavior this test guards.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Preconditions:
/// - ...
///
/// Steps:
/// - ...
///
/// Expected:
/// - ...
/// </remarks>
[Fact]
public void Build_ChunkBoundaryInsideBucket_KeepsMinimumAndMaximumOfBothChunks()
{
}
```

- A `[Theory]` is documented once; list what the cases cover under Preconditions or Steps.
- No custom XML tags. Link a design section when the why is not obvious.

## What a test checks

- A spec node is a record whose equality holds its `Id`, and `with` gives a new one: a test of a value an API builds anew (a moved annotation) compares the fields it means (the point, the range), not the nodes. PR #387's first test of an annotation put back after Escape compared the nodes and failed on the `Id` alone.
- A test of text that holds a number uses a value of the size the requirements name (a count of 10^6 rows and more, a time in nanoseconds past 2^53), not only a small one: a general format (`G6`) writes a small value as the right format does. PR #395 wrote the count of a measured range through the `double` writer, "n=1E+06" from a million rows on; its test's count was 201, and the review found it.

- A test that holds a worker on a chunk (`TestStorage.Hold`) releases it before the engine, the plan, or the pyramid is disposed: a pyramid's disposal waits for the chunk being built (design §8.1), so the test stops until the hold's watchdog. Release it in a `finally` that runs before the `using` of the engine ends. PR #294's allocation test lasted 30 s on Linux this way.
- A test that holds a chunk with `TestStorage` runs on a pool of 1 thread. `TestStorage` holds every read on a worker thread, and the frame's own M4 reads the edge elements of an unbuilt chunk (design §4.8); on a pool of several workers, a free worker takes the frame's M4 job, its read is held, and the frame waits in the fork-join until the watchdog, which real storage never makes it do. PR #329 took this for a defect of the engine (Issue #328) until the readers' stacks showed `M4.Run` and `MinMaxPyramid.QueryChunk`.
- On that pool of 1, a test whose frames extract while the held chunk's layer is drawn turns off `PlotWorkerPool.WorkersTakeJobs`, so that the caller runs every frame job. The only worker, free before it reaches the held chunk, takes the frame's M4 job first (the workers take frame jobs before chunks), reads the chunk's edge elements, and is held there: the frame waits until the watchdog, the series fails with the hold's `TimeoutException`, and the layer stays failed. `WaitForWorker` returns at once after that read, though no build is held. `HoverTests.Provisional_HitsAgainWhenItEnds` failed so once on Linux Debug, after 30 s, with a bare `Assert.True` on `IsProvisional` (Issue #589).
- On that pool of 1, a result the test needs from another job (another layer's pyramid) is built to the end before the chunk is held. The only worker may take the held chunk's job first, and no other job runs until the hold is released, so the outcome depends on the order of the jobs. PR #342's `PendingLayer_AnswersNothing` failed on Linux Debug only (Issue #345); PR #346 draws layer 0 alone until its pyramid is built, then holds the X chunk.
- A WPF test that fails locally with the front buffer unavailable (Issue #299's form: the shown texture all 0, "front buffer False") is run on main at the same time. When main fails the same way, the machine's state is the cause: the PR body says so and CI decides. In PR #352 two tests failed in three runs of the branch and two of main, and passed on CI.
- A failure set aside as another cause's is traced to that cause first (the exception, the line), and run again when `main` gets the cause's fix, with the counts read. PR #602's gate failed 5 WPF Debug tests; they were traced to a product bug, a disposed view taking input (Issue #601, fixed by PR #605), not to the machine. After `main` with the fix was merged in, those 5 passed and another test, `Disposed_UnderTheMouse_TakesTheLeave`, failed on the z-order of the windows (Issue #604, below).
- An agent's WPF test run is filtered to the tests it needs and has a timeout of 60 s: a hung run keeps its window on the screen until the timeout. A hung run is killed by the process the agent started and counted in the report as a hung run. The owner may be at the machine: an agent never moves the real cursor, and never puts a window under it. During Issue #601's work a hung run left a test window at the top left of the owner's screen, and an investigation put windows under the cursor.
- The test failures of two commits are compared at one path: both commits run alternately in the same worktree, or a run with the code and the path swapped is added. Between worktrees at different paths only the rate of failure is compared, never the names of the tests that fail. xUnit v3's `DefaultTestCaseOrderer` sorts the tests by `UniqueID`, which chains from a hash of the assembly's path, so the order of the tests differs by checkout path, and a failure that depends on the order follows the path; a file `<assembly name>.uniqueid` beside the assembly replaces the ID made from the path. PR #549 said that `DeviceLostInResize_ResizesAtTheNextFrame` failed on the branch only (13 runs of 24, none on `main`); with the code and the path swapped, 20 runs each, both sides failed 8 of 20, and the failing test followed the path (Issue #299). The fix of a test that fails only in some runs is shown by counting its cause, not only its failures: the cause is made countable first (a log line each time it happens), and the same build at the same path runs with the fix off and on, alternately. A few failures in 20 runs do not tell the two sides apart. PR #558 failed 3 runs of 20 without the window kept open and none of 20 with it (Fisher's exact test, two-sided, p = 0.23); the front buffer was lost inside a test 18 times, in 13 runs of 20, without the window and never with it, and that count showed that the cause was gone.
- A hit test's cursor in a test lies inside the panel: fractions 0 and 1 of the view, and a learned domain's end value, fall on the panel's edge, which is outside, and the hit test answers nothing. PR #360's tests failed three times so before the cause was found.
- A new test class that shares the WPF test thread goes into a collection of its own with parallelization off (`[CollectionDefinition(..., DisableParallelization = true)]`), which runs after the others; an existing class stays out of collections. PR #329 put `HyperPlotViewTests` into a named collection with the new class, and its device-loss test failed in most runs (Issue #299). A WPF test's view takes the dispatcher's backend, as the existing ones do: the new tests' own WARP backends made the view tests after them fail in 4 runs of 6.
- The null backend of the engine tests sends nothing, so the residency cache's staging grows over frames. A test that counts the bytes one frame sends says the last frame's were sent (`Images.Sent()`) before each frame, as a backend does. PR #320's test first counted the earlier frames' tiles as the frame's own.
- A test that shows one wait (a new layer waits for its pyramids) makes every other wait of the same code ready, and is checked with the mutant that drops the wait it shows. Holding the only worker stops every pass at once: in PR #311 the new layer also waited for its X statistics, so the mutant without the pyramid check passed for that reason, until the test computed the statistics the frames asked for on a second pool.
- `TestStorage.WaitForWorker` returns at once for a chunk a worker has read before; after an `Invalidate`, a test waits for `WorkerReads` of the chunk to grow. A held chunk holds every worker that reads it: two passes over the same chunk (a pyramid and a statistics pass) hold two workers, and a job queued behind them waits until the hold's watchdog. PR #311's allocation test failed so once in a run of the class; it now makes its layers before it holds the chunk.

- A test stands for the whole design statement (`.cursor/rules/spec-checks.mdc`).
- A test of values outside a domain covers both ends, below and above, and so does the design statement it checks. PR #150 drew a polar line past the outer circle and left the inner side undecided: a radius below 0 was drawn across the center, on the opposite side; the review found it, and the owner set the inner edge as the cut.
- The expected value of a subtle result (which points M4 keeps, a tick set, an interpolated color, a density cell's count) comes from an independent reference, not from reasoning: the raw data drawn or counted on the CPU, a reference implementation's output (the paper's algorithm, a library the design names), a golden image produced by an earlier reviewed run. Do not compare a function with a re-implementation of itself. An expected value written with the code's own formula copies its errors: the band ticks' test computed their pixels as the code did and missed the domain's origin both times (Issue #190); it now compares them with the middles of the bars as drawn. Nor is a reference independent when it shares the decision the test is about: another path that picks its input with the same function (which cells are in view, which rows a stat reads) agrees with the code by construction, and a defect of that function is in both. The reference is a path that does not make that decision, such as one that draws or counts every point it is given. PR #582 compared the image of the blocks in view with the image of whole pages, which chose its pages with the same `Sees`, so the test could not find that the markers of cells just past the view's edge were no longer drawn. The review found it; the new test puts a cell's boundary about half a pixel outside the edge and also compares with the CPU path, which draws every point it is given (within 6 a channel). With the margin taken out of `Sees`, that comparison differs by 213 in a channel with one part. The reference computes in a type that holds the exact result: PR #201's reference for the mean of `long` values summed in `double`, lost the low digits, and failed the correct code; it sums in `Int128`.
- Before the design takes an outside implementation as the reference for a value, make one case with it and check that it really computed that value: a reference that drops an input it does not support silently tests its default.
- A row checked against an outside reference also makes cases of its feature with each feature of an earlier row that changes the same values (a log scale with the pyramid, an append with a zoom, NaN with density), before the PR. A Risk line that names the combinations as unchecked does not replace them.
- Code written to match an outside reference is ported from the version that made the expected values.
- A branch ported from an outside implementation gets a mutant too; one HyperPlot's inputs cannot reach is removed, not kept for likeness. PR #227 ported R's `BinDist`: its left end and its range check were unreachable (the values lie in [from, to] above the grid's low end), and its right end is reached only when the range is so much wider than the bandwidth that to + 4h rounds to to, which a test now makes.
- A tool installed to make reference values is installed for the user, not the machine, and removed once its values are copied into the tests, unless the owner keeps it. PR #227 installed R 4.6.2 from CRAN's installer for the current user (winget's package is machine-wide and needs elevation) and removed it after; the tests keep R's values as a table.
- The number of reference cases that pass is read from the test run: the passed tests and the skipped ones with each reason, not from a skip reason that names only the first missing feature.
- A random test of incremental work (appends, zooms, style changes) puts several changes before one pass, and changes that keep a size but not what a later stage reads: some wrong results need two changes in one pass.
- A pixel test that draws several marks in one frame and compares them with a CPU reference keeps the marks apart, their antialiased edges included, or composes the reference in the draw order: the reference adds the colors, while the renderer composites one over the other, so a shared pixel differs.
- A test of an API that takes input calls it with the input in the form it really comes in (the host's integer or device-independent coordinates, not a pixel's center).
- A function that answers at a position from local context (a chunk lookup from an index, a bucket from a coordinate) is compared with a walk from the start, at every position of the test data, the chunk and bucket boundaries included.
- A test of code that measures or compares floating-point positions also runs at sizes and scales that are not whole (13.3 px, 125 %), and at data far from 0 (timestamps), where the conversions differ.
- A test of which interval a value falls in (a bin, a bucket, a tick) takes as values the edges themselves, computed as the code computes them, at steps that are not whole (0.1, 0.3, 0.007), not only at whole edges. A step that a double cannot hold makes the index's division and the edge's product differ by one unit in the last place. PR #219's test of bins had whole edges only; the review asked for numpy's correction, and the test of edges at steps 0.1 to 0.007 fails without it.
- A tolerance (pixel difference, float comparison) is a fixed number written in the test, with the reason in a comment when it is not the default.
- A check for NaN looks at each value: a rectangle with infinite edges (no clip) sums to NaN, since -∞ + ∞ is NaN.
- The setup that a mutant needs reaches the branch it changes, and the test asserts that it does. A cache "full" for one size may still have room for a smaller item, so the mutant that changes what fits passes unless the test checks where the next item lands.
- A memo or cache key of several values is tested by changing each value alone, the others kept: a step that changes two at once lets the mutant that drops either comparison pass. The values kept are equal to the last bit, checked in the test's arithmetic: PR #125's sqrt over [0, 100] and pow 2 over [0, √10] meant both to be [0, 10] in the scale space, the second was 10.000000000000002, the cache never hit, and the mutant that dropped the transform from the key survived; [0, 16] and [0, 2] are both [0, 4] exactly.
- A function that combines two partial results (statistics, a min/max, bounds) and has a branch for an empty side is tested with the empty side first and last, and with the other side's values all positive and all negative. The empty side's placeholder (0, `default`) lies inside one of those ranges, so a single case lets the mutant that drops the branch pass.
- A formula over doubles of the user's magnitude that squares a difference, or an integer type that a power of ten multiplies, is tested at 1e300 and 1e-300. In PR #116 the coverage term squared the range first and gave 0 or ∞, so no tick was chosen (a test found it), and writing a fixed label overflowed an Int128 (the review found it).
- A test that compares with a brute force measures its own time and keeps the gate's: PR #116's brute force made the Core tests take 12 minutes, until it skipped candidates that cannot win (which keeps its result) and ran 100 cases in 12 seconds.
- A surviving mutant is first a question about the setup (the item above). When the branch cannot be reached at all, show why and remove the branch instead of listing the mutant (`.cursor/rules/no-dead-code.mdc`).
- When a surviving mutant drops a comparison, and one side of it always holds the same value, check first whether a record is missing that value. In PR #108, the steps of polylines carried no clip, so the comparison of step kinds that kept two clips apart looked needless; the steps now carry it.
- A check that an enum value is a named one is tested with the value just past the last name (3 for `TextRotation`). −1 cast to `uint` is above every bound, so the mutant that moves the bound by one passes with it; one survived in PR #108.
- A test of a comparison (a least-recently-used pick, a priority, a tie-break) sets up candidates whose values differ; with equal values, the mutant that picks the other end passes. So does a test of which of two values a path reads (left or top, x or y): the two differ in the input. PR #142's panel sat at (0, 0), and the mutants that swapped `GeomFrame.Left` and `Top` survived.
- Two quantities the code tells apart differ in the test's input too: a parameter that makes them equal hides the mutant that reads one for the other. PR #164's bar test used a band's inner padding 0, so the band's width equalled its step, and the mutant that drew bars a step wide survived. A test of a threshold puts its input well inside each side, not on the threshold: a step placed exactly at the label width plus a quarter em let floating-point rounding decide, and the mutant that made the gap a quarter em survived; the test now places the step at three eighths of an em.
- Mutation check for a guard: change the guarded term and see the test fail, then restore it by writing the original text back (not `git checkout`), so that the file gets a new time. A file copied aside and moved back keeps its old time, the incremental build takes the mutant's DLL as current, and a later mutant in another project is measured with the earlier one still in it: PR #39 nearly counted a Core mutant's failure as a D3D11 mutant's kill. After the last mutant, build again and run the tests with no mutant; they pass. List the mutations in the PR.
- A mutant, or a trial edit, restored by copying the file back (`cp`, `mv`) gets a new time stamp (`touch`): the old one tells the incremental build that the assembly built from the mutant is newer, and the next run tests the mutant. In PR #211 a trial that disabled a fix seemed to show the fix doing nothing, until the stale build was found. PowerShell's `Copy-Item` keeps the copied file's old time too: in PR #666 a source restored with it kept the mutant's assembly in use, and the results were misread for a while. Write the text back (`[IO.File]::WriteAllText`) or set `LastWriteTime` after the copy.
- Each mutant's test run has a time limit and counts a run past it as killed: a mutant can turn a loop into one that never ends (PR #39's PNG writer, when the `IDAT` buffer was never emptied), and the script must not wait for it.
- A run stopped at its time limit is stopped with its whole process tree (`taskkill /T /F /PID <pid>`). Stopping `dotnet` alone leaves the test host it started. The host keeps the test DLLs open, and every later mutant fails to build: in PR #82, 33 mutants were run again for this.
- A mutant's text is taken from the file after CSharpier has formatted it, since a line it wraps no longer matches. A removal takes the space before it, since a space left before a comma or a parenthesis fails StyleCop; a replacement that assigns a variable to itself fails as CS1717. In PR #100, 11 of 49 mutants did not build or were not found until they were written again.
- A mutant must build. With warnings as errors, a mutant such as `if (false)` fails to compile (CS0162), `dotnet test --no-build` then runs the previous binaries, and the mutant looks survived. Check the build's exit code before reading the test result, and write the mutant so that it compiles (`if (IsActive && count < 0)`). A style analyzer fails the build too (a trailing space is SA1028). A mutant run that names no failing test counts as a failed build until its build output says otherwise.
- A step that can leave state for the next item (a coverage texture, a scratch buffer, a cache line) is tested with a next item where that state would show. PR #47's scissor mutant survived: coverage written outside a clip was never cleared, and no test drew a later line across it.
- A test waits for the state its next step relies on, not for one that comes before it. PR #55's test waited for `HasWork`, which turns true before the batch is in the list the workers read, and the release that followed could reach the worker first.
- A rule that picks one item among several (the smallest failed number, the first match, the newest entry) is tested with an input where the edge item is the one picked. PR #55 tested "the smallest failed series" with series 1 and 3 failing, and a mutant that skipped series 0 survived.
- A mutant is measured by the tests of the project its file is in and of the projects that use it. PR #47's script ran only the D3D11 tests, also for mutants of Core's `RenderList`, and reported two as survived that Core's tests kill.
- A mutant runs first the test classes of what it changes (`--filter-class`), and the whole suites only when those pass: a kill by the subset is a kill, and a survivor still meets the projects that use the file. Mutants with no build output in common may run in two worktrees side by side, never while a measurement runs. Row G10's 39 mutants (Issue #140) took close to an hour one after another against the whole Core suite each; the owner asked for this order.
- A script that runs mutants decides from the exit code of `dotnet test`, not from its text: Microsoft.Testing.Platform exits with 2 when a test failed, and its messages follow the OS language. `python -I` ignores `PYTHONIOENCODING`; a script that prints test output calls `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")`.
- A mutant that would dereference null or read out of range in unsafe code is not run: it ends the test process and the other tests do not run. List it in the PR with that reason. A mutant that only leaks a COM reference cannot be caught without the D3D11 debug layer; say so too.
- Every test of a contract the design states gets that mutation check before the PR, not only the guards a review names. A test that passes against the mutant passes for another reason, and the reason is usually the test's own setup or cleanup.
- A test of a fix says which of its assertions guards the fix, and that one fails on the code before the fix or on a mutant that undoes it. An assertion that passes on the old code guards something else, which the remarks then name. PR #465's hit assertion passed on the old code (the old buffers held the whole column); the mutant that drops the row offset showed it guards the new indexing, and the new `MappedRows` assertion guards the fix.
- An allocation test, and any test that passes when nothing happens, also asserts that the work ran: no layer failed, the frame drew what it should. PR #480's flipped horizontal-rule case passed at 0 B because unsorted y made the rule's layer fail and draw nothing.
- After a change of behavior, the tests that check the old behavior are found before any test runs: search `tests/` for the members and the limits the change touches, and run those classes too, not only the classes of the changed files. PR #497 made a full color table reuse its oldest row and ran the density and cache tests only; `ImageGeomTests` still expected the old exception and failed on CI.
- Code that reads an input (a file format, a header, arguments) lists every branch that rejects one before its mutants are picked, and each branch gets a test and a mutant, also those the design's test row does not name. PR #71 picked the decoder's mutants from the forms §10 lists, and its branches for a missing `IDAT` and data that does not inflate had neither; the review found them.
- A test that waits for an asynchronous result (a GPU query on WARP, a worker's chunk) draws or polls until the result arrives, with a bound, not for a fixed number of steps; and it sets its hooks before the first call that can produce one. PR #63's `FrameTests` waited 20 frames for the times of frames 0 and 1 and set the hold after `CreateRenderTarget`, which opens frame 0 and reads it as it closes: they passed on the reference machine and failed on the shared Windows runner, where WARP finished sooner or later than here.
- A test that reads a hook's records after waiting for a completion (`Completion`, a task) waits for the records themselves when the hook can run after that completion. `PlotWorkerPool` calls `TestTook` after a chunk is built, so the last chunk's record can come after the pyramid completes; PR #91 fixed a test that failed on it once on Windows.
- A fix of a test that fails now and then is checked by widening the race on purpose (a sleep inside the hook, put in for the check and taken out): the old test fails as the CI did, and the fixed one passes (PR #91). A run of passes is not that check: 300 passes of a 1-in-100 failure are about 95 % sure. PR #307 made it a test: another thread holds the pool's lock through a measured extraction (`HoldGate`), and a pool without the warm-up counts 56 bytes there, one with it 0.
- A hook that makes a test take a branch enters the branch the real condition enters, not a branch of its own. PR #63's `TestHold` returned before the readiness check, so the mutant that skipped a set not yet ready survived; the hook now sits inside the same condition as `GetData`. A path the tests reach only through such a hook (a seam that sets a state) is also run once through the real state, made the way the OS or the host makes it: a seam sets a value and does none of what the real event does besides. `LostFrontBuffer_ReadsBackAndComesBack` lost the front buffer through the `FrontBuffer` seam, so no test saw that the view's `SetBackBuffer(null)` detached the `D3DImage` from its bitmap, after which WPF sent no notice that the front buffer was back, and the view stayed on the read-back path (0 of 11 losses came back, Issue #556). PR #588's `FrontBufferLostByWpf_ReadsBackAndComesBack` sets `RenderOptions.ProcessRenderMode` to software and back, so that WPF sends its own notices, and fails on the code before the fix.
- Read the state you assert before a shared helper's cleanup runs. A helper that resets state on the way out makes an assertion after it check the helper, not the code. Record the state inside the run, where the code under test left it.

```csharp
// BAD — RunFrames' finally stops the render loop: this passes even when the frame never stops it
RunFrames(host, frames: 1);
Assert.False(host.IsRendering);

// GOOD — read the state inside the run, after the frame and before the cleanup
bool? renderingAfterFrame = null;
RunFrames(host, frames: 1, afterFrame: () => renderingAfterFrame = host.IsRendering);
Assert.False(renderingAfterFrame);
```

A row that adds states that workers move through runs its tests three times in a row while the machine is loaded (a benchmark or another test run beside them) before the PR. Row C3a's two races, the order of completion and a group read while it was being laid out, showed only when the tests ran beside the 1e9-point measurement. A race that no test forces is listed in the PR body and gets an Issue (Issue #410).

A mutant is undone by writing the saved text of the file back, not by `git checkout <file>`: the checkout also discards the uncommitted edits of that file. In PR #415 it took a comment's fix with it, which had to be made again.

Tests run only after a build that succeeded. With `dotnet build ... | grep error; dotnet test --no-build`, a build that an analyzer stopped leaves the old binaries, and the tests report on those: in PR #415 a failure "stayed" after its fix until the blank line that the analyzer asked for was added. Chain the two with `&&` on the build's exit code, or read the build's result first.
